WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.323
Welcome to Lecture 12 of the

2
00:00:02.395 --> 00:00:04.780
FrontierCS 2 course, on security.

3
00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.638
FrontierCS 2 takes a research paper

4
00:00:07.708 --> 00:00:09.450
removes its key idea from

5
00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:11.053
the authors' own code

6
00:00:11.053 --> 00:00:13.770
and asks an AI agent to write it back.

7
00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:16.600
This lecture covers two such tasks.

8
00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:19.472
LRR repairs regular expressions

9
00:00:19.553 --> 00:00:21.750
that an attacker can stall.

10
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.955
Progent stops a tool-using agent from obeying

11
00:00:25.019 --> 00:00:27.620
instructions hidden in the data it reads.

12
00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.498
A regular expression, or regex

13
00:00:30.498 --> 00:00:33.240
checks an input in one line of code.

14
00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:36.380
Most regex engines, Python's among them

15
00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:37.984
match by backtracking

16
00:00:37.984 --> 00:00:39.588
when one choice fails

17
00:00:39.588 --> 00:00:41.750
they go back and try the next.

18
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.025
For some patterns and some inputs

19
00:00:44.081 --> 00:00:46.402
the number of choices grows exponentially

20
00:00:46.458 --> 00:00:48.100
with the length of the input.

21
00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:50.242
Then a few dozen characters

22
00:00:50.309 --> 00:00:51.570
can stall a server.

23
00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.749
This attack is called regular expression

24
00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:56.800
denial of service, or ReDoS.

25
00:00:57.380 --> 00:00:59.552
To see it, we wrote a small matcher

26
00:00:59.614 --> 00:01:00.980
that counts its steps.

27
00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:04.370
Take two patterns for one or more letters a.

28
00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:06.800
The first is a-plus.

29
00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:09.224
The second puts a-plus in a group

30
00:01:09.287 --> 00:01:10.670
and repeats the group.

31
00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.140
Feed each one n letters a

32
00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:15.260
then an exclamation mark.

33
00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:16.707
At four letters

34
00:01:16.776 --> 00:01:19.176
the nested pattern takes sixty-four

35
00:01:19.244 --> 00:01:21.850
steps to fail, and the simple one six.

36
00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:22.843
At eight

37
00:01:22.843 --> 00:01:25.560
one thousand twenty-four against ten.

38
00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:28.670
At sixteen, two hundred sixty-two thousand

39
00:01:28.736 --> 00:01:31.430
one hundred forty-four, against eighteen.

40
00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:33.610
Pause and predict.

41
00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.760
About how many steps at seventeen letters?

42
00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:41.550
About twice as many

43
00:01:41.612 --> 00:01:43.658
five hundred twenty-four thousand

44
00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:45.270
two hundred eighty-eight.

45
00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:48.180
Every extra letter doubles the work.

46
00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:50.780
Why does it explode?

47
00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:52.770
The exclamation mark makes

48
00:01:52.833 --> 00:01:54.032
every attempt fail

49
00:01:54.095 --> 00:01:56.745
so the matcher must try every way to match

50
00:01:56.808 --> 00:01:58.890
the letters before it can say no.

51
00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:01.729
Three letters can be cut into

52
00:02:01.815 --> 00:02:04.475
groups in four ways: all three

53
00:02:04.475 --> 00:02:07.564
two then one, one then two, or one

54
00:02:07.564 --> 00:02:08.680
one and one.

55
00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:10.826
Eight letters can be cut in one

56
00:02:10.884 --> 00:02:12.390
hundred twenty-eight ways.

57
00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:14.740
The pattern is ambiguous

58
00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:16.980
one string has many parses.

59
00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:19.725
Ambiguity under a repeat is the

60
00:02:19.802 --> 00:02:21.810
root of exponential ReDoS.

61
00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:25.088
A repair must remove the ambiguity and

62
00:02:25.159 --> 00:02:27.430
accept exactly the same strings.

63
00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:28.980
We check both.

64
00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:31.465
The nested pattern followed

65
00:02:31.544 --> 00:02:34.232
by b becomes a-plus, then b: safe

66
00:02:34.232 --> 00:02:36.050
and the same language.

67
00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:39.619
But another repair, a group of a or b

68
00:02:39.619 --> 00:02:42.668
repeated, then b, also accepts b-b

69
00:02:42.668 --> 00:02:45.040
which the original rejects.

70
00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:47.150
It widened the language.

71
00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:50.405
For a password rule or an input filter

72
00:02:50.405 --> 00:02:51.820
that is a new bug.

73
00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.177
So: backtracking tries every way to read

74
00:02:55.246 --> 00:02:57.120
the string before it fails.

75
00:02:57.470 --> 00:02:59.310
Ambiguity under a repeat

76
00:02:59.387 --> 00:03:01.150
means exponential time.

77
00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:04.060
And a repair must keep the language.

78
00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:05.558
As an exercise

79
00:03:05.558 --> 00:03:09.001
count the steps of a-star a-star b at eight

80
00:03:09.001 --> 00:03:11.450
sixteen and thirty-two letters.

81
00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:13.275
It is ambiguous too

82
00:03:13.275 --> 00:03:15.560
but it grows much more slowly.

83
00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:19.074
Next, LRR: how a small language model

84
00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:20.870
makes these repairs.
